Ladybird’s webRequest namespace takes its shape from the Firefox
build that the extensions this stage targets are written for:
the nine events, the enumerations with each value under its name
uppercased (so OnBeforeRequestOptions.REQUESTBODY, as Firefox’s
Schemas.sys.mjs builds them), and addListener()’s validation and
error messages, which are copied word for word since extensions
compare them: “Incorrect argument types”, “Type error for parameter
filter (...)”, and the blocking-permission message from Firefox’s
webRequestBlockingPermissionRequired postprocessor.

Firefox drops a filter type it doesn’t know with a warning (the types
items carry onError “warn”) and throws only when none remain, and
it checks URL patterns in its parent process (parseMatchPatterns in
ext-webRequest.js), so a bad pattern is logged rather than thrown;
Ladybird does both the same way. Ladybird also accepts “extraHeaders”,
which Firefox would reject: Chromium needs it (WebRequestAPI’s
OnBeforeSendHeadersOptions has EXTRA_HEADERS), and uBlock Origin
adds it whenever it doesn’t detect Firefox. It isn’t added to the
enumeration, so Bypass Paywalls Clean’s probe for EXTRA_HEADERS stays
false, as in Firefox. WebKit has no webRequest; its extensions use
declarativeNetRequest only.
